{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.Hecke.BondCutSymmetry","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.Hecke.BondCutSymmetry","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic"],"declarations":17,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Hecke.BondCutSymmetry.flavour_tilt_wrong_direction","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Hecke.BondCutSymmetry.flavour_tilt_wrong_direction","kind":"theorem","line":313,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Hecke/BondCutSymmetry.lean#L313-L326","doc":"**The flavour tilt pushes the weight the wrong way.** The one derived\nsub-leading ingredient in `w = 1/2 + δ_f/4` is the p–n flavour split\n`δ_f = Δm_pn/M_N`, whose sign is fixed by the neutron being heavier:\n`δ_f > 0`. It moves `w` *up*, while the crossing `1/(q²+1)` sits *below* `1/2`\n(`SymmetrySignAmGm.crossing_lt_half`). So the miss is larger with the derived\ncorrection than without it. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Hecke.BondCutSymmetry.weight_miss_ge_half_hbar","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Hecke.BondCutSymmetry.weight_miss_ge_half_hbar","kind":"theorem","line":301,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Hecke/BondCutSymmetry.lean#L301-L311","doc":"**The miss against the crossing is first order in `ℏ_q`.** With\n`ℏ_q = (q-1)/q`, the deformation part of `SymmetrySignAmGm.weight_miss`\nsatisfies `(q²-1)/(2(q²+1)) ≥ ℏ_q/2` for every `q ≥ 1` — the difference is\n`(q-1)²/(2q(q²+1)) ≥ 0`. So a correction of order `ℏ_q²` cannot close it unless\nits coefficient is at least `1/(2ℏ_q)`, which at `q₀` is about `5`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Hecke.BondCutSymmetry.bondSum_ne_semf_neutronMatter","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Hecke.BondCutSymmetry.bondSum_ne_semf_neutronMatter","kind":"theorem","line":282,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Hecke/BondCutSymmetry.lean#L282-L297","doc":"**No pair-additive bond count with non-negative energies agrees with the\nSEMF at pure neutron matter** — on any graph, with any interface, at any\nscale `E₀ > 0`. The left side is `≥ 0` and the right side is `< 0`. This is the\nlayout-free form of \"the bond count cannot supply the symmetry energy\": the\nmagnitude the data demands is the magnitude that makes neutron matter unbound,\nand positive bond energies cannot unbind anything. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Hecke.BondCutSymmetry.semf_neutronMatter_neg","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Hecke.BondCutSymmetry.semf_neutronMatter_neg","kind":"theorem","line":270,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Hecke/BondCutSymmetry.lean#L270-L280","doc":"**The SEMF with `a_A > a_V` unbinds neutron matter.** At `Z = 0` the\nasymmetry `(N-Z)²/A` equals `A`, so the volume and symmetry terms combine to\n`(a_V - a_A)·A < 0`. The surface and pairing terms only lower it further.\n`a_A > a_V` is what AME2020 measures (`≈ 23` against `≈ 16` MeV); it enters\nhere as a hypothesis, never as a fit. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Hecke.BondCutSymmetry.bondSum_nonneg","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Hecke.BondCutSymmetry.bondSum_nonneg","kind":"theorem","line":257,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Hecke/BondCutSymmetry.lean#L257-L268","doc":"**A bond sum is non-negative at every composition.** Non-negative energies\nagainst non-negative counts. Pure neutron matter (`Z = 0`, `n_pp = n_pn = 0`)\nis a composition like any other, so the law binds it. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Hecke.BondCutSymmetry.cut_le_degree_bound","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Hecke.BondCutSymmetry.cut_le_degree_bound","kind":"theorem","line":244,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Hecke/BondCutSymmetry.lean#L244-L255","doc":"**The cut is bounded by the smaller colour class times the degree bound.**\nFrom the degree-sum identities with non-negative like-species counts and a\ndegree bound `d`, `n_pn ≤ d·Z` and `n_pn ≤ d·N`. So on any bounded-degree\ngraph the layout-dependent part `δ·n_pn` is at most `|δ|·d·min(Z, N)` — a\nchain (`d = 2`) can move the law by at most `2|δ|·min(Z,N)`, which is linear\nin `|N-Z|` at fixed `A`, never quadratic. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Hecke.BondCutSymmetry.symmetryCoeff_pos_iff","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Hecke.BondCutSymmetry.symmetryCoeff_pos_iff","kind":"theorem","line":221,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Hecke/BondCutSymmetry.lean#L221-L242","doc":"**The SEMF sign is `δ > 0`.** The symmetry coefficient is positive — the\nlaw binds symmetric nuclei more — exactly when the p–n energy exceeds the\narithmetic mean of the like-species energies. With `e = -ln b` that is\n`b_pn < √(b_pp b_nn)`: `SymmetrySignAmGm.delta_nonpos_iff`, with the power\nattached. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Hecke.BondCutSymmetry.bondSum_wellMixed_symmetry","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Hecke.BondCutSymmetry.bondSum_wellMixed_symmetry","kind":"theorem","line":205,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Hecke/BondCutSymmetry.lean#L205-L211","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Hecke.BondCutSymmetry.symmetryCoeff","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Hecke.BondCutSymmetry.symmetryCoeff","kind":"def","line":200,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Hecke/BondCutSymmetry.lean#L200-L203","doc":"The coefficient of `-(N-Z)²` in the well-mixed law: `a_A`-like, positive\nmeans the SEMF sign. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Hecke.BondCutSymmetry.bondSum_wellMixed","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Hecke.BondCutSymmetry.bondSum_wellMixed","kind":"theorem","line":186,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Hecke/BondCutSymmetry.lean#L186-L198","doc":"**The well-mixed (mean-field) layout carries exactly one symmetry term.**\nWith cut `n_pn = μ·Z·N` on a `k`-regular graph, the bond sum is the like-species\nline plus `δμ/4` times `(A² - (N-Z)²)`: the mixing contribution to the volume\nterm and the symmetry term have the *same* coefficient, opposite signs.\n\nThis is the only place the mean-field model enters. It is a model, not a\ntheorem about the braid: it is what \"well-mixed\" means. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Hecke.BondCutSymmetry.bondSum_segregated_sub","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Hecke.BondCutSymmetry.bondSum_segregated_sub","kind":"theorem","line":174,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Hecke/BondCutSymmetry.lean#L174-L184","doc":"**A constant cut carries no symmetry term.** On a `k`-regular graph whose\np–n cut is a constant `c` (every segregated layout; the chain has `c = 1`),\nthe difference of the bond sums at two compositions is *linear* in the\ncomposition difference, with only the like-species energies — for every\n`e_pp, e_pn, e_nn`. There is no `(N-Z)²` to have a sign. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Hecke.BondCutSymmetry.bondSum_chain","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Hecke.BondCutSymmetry.bondSum_chain","kind":"theorem","line":164,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Hecke/BondCutSymmetry.lean#L164-L172","doc":"**The canonical `A-1` chain, as an instance.** Segregated `p…p n…n` with\n`Z-1` p–p edges, one p–n edge and `N-1` n–n edges is the corpus's law\n`(Z-1)e_pp + e_pn + (N-1)e_nn`; its endpoint degrees are `2Z-1` and `2N-1`\nand its cut is `1`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Hecke.BondCutSymmetry.bondSum_eq_cut","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Hecke.BondCutSymmetry.bondSum_eq_cut","kind":"theorem","line":154,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Hecke/BondCutSymmetry.lean#L154-L162","doc":"**The cut identity.** The bond sum is the like-species degree sum, halved,\nplus the p–n cut times the slope `δ`. The whole layout dependence is `δ · n_pn`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Hecke.BondCutSymmetry.energySlope","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Hecke.BondCutSymmetry.energySlope","kind":"def","line":147,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Hecke/BondCutSymmetry.lean#L147-L152","doc":"The p–n slope: the change in the bond sum when one like-species pair of\nhalf-bonds is traded for a p–n bond. In the corpus's letters this is\n`SymmetrySignAmGm.delta` with `e = -ln b` (`BondCutSymmetryLogBound.energySlope_eq_log_form`).\nNeeds division by `2`, hence a field. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Hecke.BondCutSymmetry.four_mul_ZN","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Hecke.BondCutSymmetry.four_mul_ZN","kind":"theorem","line":134,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Hecke/BondCutSymmetry.lean#L134-L137","doc":"`4ZN = (Z+N)² - (N-Z)²`: the mean-field cut is a difference of the volume\nsquare and the asymmetry square, with equal and opposite coefficients. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Hecke.BondCutSymmetry.two_mul_bondSum","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Hecke.BondCutSymmetry.two_mul_bondSum","kind":"theorem","line":121,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Hecke/BondCutSymmetry.lean#L121-L132","doc":"**The cut identity, ring form.** If the proton and neutron colour classes\nhave total degrees `d_P` and `d_N` — the degree-sum formula on each class,\n`2 n_pp + n_pn = d_P`, `2 n_nn + n_pn = d_N` — then twice the bond sum is the\nlike-species degree sum plus the cut weighted by `2 e_pn - e_pp - e_nn`. No\ngraph structure beyond the two degree sums is used. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Hecke.BondCutSymmetry.bondSum","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Hecke.BondCutSymmetry.bondSum","kind":"def","line":115,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Hecke/BondCutSymmetry.lean#L115-L119","doc":"A pair-additive bond-counting law: three per-bond energies against three\npair counts. Under Option B the energies are `e_t = -ln b_t` and the counts\nare read off a bond graph; nothing below depends on either reading. ","signatureChunk":0}]}